have they extended the terms with other creditors?

Projected cash flow for the proposed term of the payment arrangement	
Used for providing a measure of an entity's liquidity by recording cash receipts and payments showing the net changes in cash for a given period.
Did the taxpayer include the payment proposal amounts to the ATO in their forecast?
Do payments and receivables match the debtors and creditors list provided?
Most recent statement for each financial institution account held	
Bank statements of the last three months can:

confirm if the financial position the entity claims to be in is accurate
show whether there are large sums of money being moved out of an account. This is to show a smaller 'cash at hand' in their payment proposal
indicate whether they are still receiving funds from trade debtors on a regular basis.
Detailed balance sheet or statement of financial position	
This gives you an idea of the entity's financial position. It displays what the entity owns and owes.
You should be able to identify if tax obligations (Super/PAYGW/GST) have been accounted for and included in their costs.
Details of any overdraft or loan facilities, including balances owing and monthly repayments	
Provides information on whether the taxpayer can

draw down to finance Payment in Full (PIF) or

manage larger instalments to reduce the payment plan timeframe.

Repayment amounts need to also be considered in the cashflow to accurately determine ability to

pay the ATO debt, and

meet their ongoing obligations.

It may also identify if there are inter- or intra-company loans. This

may also be found on bank transactions

may assist with considerations that ultimate holding company (UHC) or Group Head has capacity to pay the debts

is also relevant if the taxpayer is part of a Consolidated Group.

Details of current contracts / future works	
May provide information on the taxpayers current and future income streams.
Are these contracts finalised and/or guaranteed?
Are they conditional contracts (i.e. payment only when a service or defect target has been met also known as a retention fund usually is 10%)?
Can you match this amount on the cashflow provided (possibly through payment time frames or information from the contract deliverable dates?
Have they structured entities where one company holds the employees who are 'charged out' to other companies holding the contracts and assets?
Positive cash flow
It is important for businesses to have positive cash flow. Without positive cashflow, it is difficult for the taxpayer to:

run their business, and
be able to pay their bills on time when they have insufficient funds available at their disposal.
Therefore, the taxpayer will need to assess, revaluate and look at changing their business structures before it is too late. For example, some options available for the taxpayer is for the directors to:

inject cash/wealth into the business, e.g. director loans

look at

refinancing

obtaining a business loan

applying for or increasing an existing overdraft facility (if it is currently maxed out or overdrawn)

look at freeing up some capital by selling off some assets. The aim is to free up working capital to allow the business to be sustainable to

be able to continue to trade, and

have capacity to pay its bills on time or when called upon.

Negative cash flow
Negative cash flow is when a business spends more cash than it generates in a particular period. This implies the total cash inflow is less than total outflow from the following activities:

operating activities
investing activities
financing activities.
It is a prevalent situation for businesses in their growth phase as they need to spend money to:

fuel growth
acquire customers, or
set up distribution channels.
In such situation, the deficit should be supported by equity infusion, debt funding, or both.

Insolvent
A business is insolvent when it:

has no liquid or tangible assets that it can sell, borrow or refinance against, and
is not in a position to be able to pay when it is being called upon to pay.
If insolvent, the business should seek legal advice from an insolvency practitioner or specialist regarding managing debt.Opens in a new window If you are unsure, seek advice from your coach.

The financials can safeguard the ATO from having to repay preference payments to the insolvency practitioner. This is because the financials show that the entity was viable at the time the P/A was negotiated.

