Remissions
Information
The remission procedure enables frontline staff to consider requests from sophisticated taxpayers where the:

request meets low value activity guidelines criteria, or
value of the request is $10,000 or less for each GIC and FTL penalty.
To consider remission of interest or penalties:

the taxpayer needs to have taken reasonable steps to try and mitigate the effects of the circumstances
we may also take into consideration special circumstances. For example, where a taxpayer
has a good payment history, and
is late on a one-off basis or on an isolated occasion.
Follow Telephony request - GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellationOpens in a new window or Correspondence request - GIC, FTL, SIC remission or cancellation.Opens in a new window Use the below information as a guide.

Go to the relevant sections:

Low value guidelines
GIC remission
FTL on Time Penalty remission
SIC remission
Related resources.
Low value guidelines
Remission requests for sophisticated taxpayers may be granted if the taxpayers request meets the low-risk and good compliance history guidelines. Refer to low value activity guidelines.Opens in a new window

GIC remission
GIC may be remitted on Super Guarantee Employer Reporting accounts (SGER) in exceptional circumstances.

If an employer does not pay their employee's super on time and to the right superfund, the employer must:

pay the superannuation guarantee charge (SGC)
lodge an SGC statement with the ATO.
The liability is then raised and is reported in the SGER account. It is important to note that:

the SGC is payable by employers who failed to provide the prescribed minimum level of superannuation support by the due date

the amount is then collected on behalf of the employees by the ATO. This is to compensate the employees for their lost earnings to safeguard the employees retirement.

Considerations (for all GIC remission requests):

What has caused the taxpayer inability to be able to pay on time?

Were the circumstances unexpected and beyond taxpayer control? (e.g. natural disaster, unforeseen collapse of a major debtor)?

Was the delay in payment caused by the taxpayer actions or from failure to act?

Did the taxpayer act within a reasonable timeframe to reduce the severity and impact of the circumstances? For example, by

notifying the ATO

keeping the ATO informed

calling to negotiate a sustainable P/A, or

making voluntary payments.

Does the taxpayer have a good lodgment and payment history?

Was the late payment a one-off event (e.g. an isolated incident)?

Does the taxpayer have a history of making late payments including sending in requests for GIC remission?

Was the delay in payment caused by the taxpayer?

Would an ordinary member of the community who pay their taxes on time see the circumstances as fair and reasonable to grant the GIC remission?

When making your decision, consider the size and resources of the taxpayer have. They are likely to have in-house accountants, tax staff and external professional advisors at their disposal.

Is the taxpayer part of a larger economic group?

Depending on the amount and circumstances of the remission request, further profiling may be necessary to understand the economic group.

The groups resource may need to be considered when making the decision. It is important to check the group for additional unactioned GIC remission requests to action under Whole of Group.

In some cases, a call to the authorised contact may be required to

request additional information, or

seek further clarification to on the taxpayer's inability to pay. For example, to establish timelines of events.

Failure to Lodge (FTL) on Time Penalty remission
You should generally only remit the FTL penalty:

after the document is lodged, and
when an FTL penalty has been applied to the taxpayer account.
Considerations:

What has caused the taxpayer inability to be able to lodge on time?
Were the circumstances unexpected and beyond the taxpayer control? For example, they were unable to access to financial information due to
natural disaster
court case
collapse of a major debtor
them waiting on a private ruling.
Was the delay in lodgment caused by the taxpayer own actions from failure to act?
Did the taxpayer act in a reasonable timeframe to try and reduce the severity and impact of the circumstances? For example, by
notifying the ATO
keeping the ATO informed, or
calling to request a deferral or suspension.
Does the taxpayer have a good lodgment and payment history?
Was the late lodgment a one-off event? For example, an isolated incident.
Does the taxpayer have a history of
making late lodgments including requests for deferrals/suspensions, or
applying for FTL penalty remission requests?
Would an ordinary member of the community who lodges and pays their taxes on time see the circumstances as fair and reasonable to grant the FTL penalty remission?
When making your decision, consider the size and resources of the taxpayer. They are likely to have in-house accountants, tax staff and external professional advisors at their disposal.
Is the taxpayer part of a larger economic group?
Depending on the amount and circumstances of the FTL penalty remission request, further profiling may be necessary to get a better understanding of the economic group.
The groups resource may need to be considered when making the decision. It is important to check the group for additional unactioned FTL penalty remission requests to action under Whole of Group.
In some cases, a call to the authorised contact may be required to
request additional information, or
to seek further clarification.
SIC remission
SIC is a compound interest imposed on income tax shortfalls from 2004-2005 financial years onwards. An income tax shortfall arises when a debit amendment increases the taxpayer's income tax liability. SIC is payable on the additional amount of income tax, i.e. the tax shortfall (TSF) amount. The shortfall period is the period from the:

payment due date of the original assessment, or
the notional due date to the day before the Commissioner gives the taxpayer a notice of amended assessment (NOAA).
The SIC will need to be manually adjusted if:

a taxpayer lodges subsequent amendment, and
this subsequent amendment adjusts the same particulars as an amendment that has had SIC applied.
Considerations:

SIC replaces GIC during the shortfall period and is calculated at a lower rate than GIC.
What has caused the taxpayer inability to be able to lodge the correct information when the ITA return was due?
Were the circumstances unexpected and beyond taxpayer control? For example,
natural disaster
unforeseen collapse of a major debtor
the taxpayer was waiting on a private ruling.
Was the delay in lodgment caused by the taxpayer actions or from failure to act where the information was amended due to an ATO audit?
Did the taxpayer act within a reasonable timeframe to reduce the severity and impact of the circumstances? For example, by
notifying the ATO
keeping the ATO informed
advising
incorrect information has been lodged on the ITA return, and
an amendment will need to be lodged and sent in
calling in to negotiate a sustainable P/A
making voluntary payments.
The taxpayer is entitled to a remission of SIC where an early payment has been made.
SIC should be reduced by the amount of any interest on early payment (IEP) that the taxpayer is entitled to.
Does the taxpayer have a good lodgment and payment history?
Was the late lodgment resulting in an amendment a one-off event? For example an isolated incident.
Does the taxpayer have a history of
lodging the incorrect information
amending the ITA return, and then
sending in a request for SIC remission?
Was the delay in lodging the ITA return caused by the taxpayer?
Would an ordinary member of the community who pay their taxes on time see the circumstances as fair and reasonable to grant the SIC remission?
When making your decision, consider the size and resources of the taxpayer have. They are likely to have in-house accountants, tax staff and external professional advisors at their disposal.
Is the taxpayer part of a larger economic group?
Depending on the amount and circumstances of the remission request, further profiling may be necessary to understand the economic group.
The groups resource may need to be considered when making the decision. It is important to check the group for additional unactioned SIC remission requests to action under Whole of Group.
In some cases, a call to the authorised contact may be required to seek further information/clarification. This is to find out the taxpayer's inability to lodge the correct information when the original tax return was lodged.
