Deferrals
Refer to the Know your Sophisticated Taxpayer Guide,Opens in a new window Deferral and Suspension guide for Sophisticated TaxpayersOpens in a new window and Deferral and suspensionsOpens in a new window to:

determine the risk associated with granting or refusing the deferral request
look at how the outcome would affect both the ATO and the taxpayer. This would include, but is not limited to
the risk of external reviews, complaints, and risk to the ATO reputation, and
for the taxpayer, the risk of
an unfair financial advantage
disengagement from these taxpayers
insolvency.
While considering the above information, you will need to:

ensure these requests are actioned separately for each taxpayer, as they are legally separate entities
still understand and evaluate any potential group level of the SC approach.
When deciding whether it is fair and reasonable to grant a deferral, consider:

the reason why the entity or their representative is unable to lodge on time

the value of the information provided in the document

the size and structure of the entity. Large corporate entities are more likely to have the ability and available resources to overcome circumstances that might affect their ability to not lodge by the due date

the risk to revenue

the entity's compliance history as a whole, that is

lodgment of taxation returns, activity statements and other documents

payments on time

previous dealings with the ATO

the length of time needed to lodge the document. A deferral will usually be granted where an entity

has a good compliance history, and

requests a short period of additional time to lodge

any other relevant information that includes the individual circumstances.

We generally consider it to be fair and reasonable to grant a deferral where the inability to lodge by the due date is reasonably attributed to exceptional or unforeseen circumstances. These include:

natural disasters or other disasters or events that may have, or have had, a significant impact on individuals, regions, or particular industries
impeded access to records (for example, records seized during a police search or retained as evidence in a court matter)
the serious illness or death of a family member, tax professional or critical staff member
considerable lack of knowledge and understanding of taxation obligations
system issues, either with ATO online services or the entity's business system.
Complete the following before you contact the taxpayer to advise of the outcome or request additional information:

assess the request following relevant
the ATO guidelines
EKM procedures
practice statements (PSLA), and
profile the taxpayer (including associated group/entities)
consider the risk.
If the taxpayer is a Large Withholder and/or Significant Excise Payer, deferral for payments of PAYGW, GST or Excise is not available. This is unless the company can demonstrate:

they do not have access to alternate sources of funding, and

that meeting their payment obligations would be a contributing factor in their becoming insolvent in the immediate future.

Registered agent requests
Registered agents can submit requests for deferral of:

lodgment, or lodgment and payment, via Online services for agents (OSfA)

using the lodgment deferralOpens in a new window function, or

